	 <div class="cca-help" id="nc-cca-help-modal" hidden>
		  <div class="cca-help-card">
				<button class="x" id="nc-cca-help-close" title="Close">&#10005;</button>
				<h2>Card Combination Analyser &mdash; what am I looking at?</h2>
				<p>It estimates, for one partnership on <b>this</b> deal, how many <b>tricks</b> they can win &mdash;
					suit by suit and in total &mdash; given the cards they hold and all the ways the opponents' cards
					might be split. It is a quick guide to "how high can we go?", not the last word.</p>
				<h3>The table</h3>
				<p>Each row <b>&spades; &hearts; &diams; &clubs;</b> is one suit, with a small
					<span style="color:#2f6fd8"><b>sd</b></span> sub-row under it. The black row is the
					double-dummy ceiling; the blue <span style="color:#2f6fd8"><b>sd</b></span> row is the same
					spread when you play the recommended line <i>blind</i> &mdash; it sits left of (worse than)
					the black row, the shift being the price of not seeing the cards. The columns
					<code>0&hellip;13</code> are the chance of taking <i>exactly</i> that many tricks in the suit.
					The <b>E</b> column is the average number of tricks &mdash;
					<b>double-dummy / <span style="color:#2f6fd8">blind</span></b>
					(the blue figure is what you'd take playing blind, always the smaller). The <b>line</b> column
					is the suggested way to play that suit blind: <i>cash</i> (bang out top cards), <i>finesse</i>,
					or <i>duck</i> (give up an early round). It is a simplified pick from a few standard plays &mdash;
					a real best line can <i>combine</i> them (e.g. duck a round <i>then</i> finesse), which the one
					word can't show, so read it as the general idea, not an exact recipe. <b>Hover the line word</b>
					for a fuller, cards-specific plan.</p>
				<h3>Two answers: ceiling vs. realistic</h3>
				<p><span class="sw" style="background:#222"></span><b>tot</b> and <b>&ge;k</b> (black) &mdash; the
					<b>double-dummy</b> ceiling: how it would go if you could <i>see all four hands</i>. Best case /
					hindsight &mdash; usually better than real life. These percentages are <b>not about the one split
					you see on this deal</b>: they average double-dummy play (all four hands seen) over <i>every</i>
					way the opponents' cards could be divided, weighted by how likely each split is. So it answers
					"across all possible layouts of their cards, how often does the suit yield this many tricks?",
					not "on this exact layout".</p>
				<p><span class="sw" style="background:#2f6fd8"></span><b>sd</b> and <b>&ge;sd</b> (blue) &mdash; the
					<b>single-dummy</b>, realistic figure: playing <i>blind</i> to the opponents' cards, so you must
					guess (finesses, which way to play). This is closer to what you'll actually take. Like the black
					figure it also averages over <i>every</i> possible opponent split (weighted by likelihood), not
					the one on this deal &mdash; the only difference is that here you <b>play blind</b>, picking one
					line in advance without seeing their cards, so it counts the times a guess goes wrong.</p>
				<p>The gap between black and blue is the price of not seeing the cards. <b>&ge;sd</b> is the best you
					can do by choosing the smartest line in each suit for the target you set below.</p>
				<h3>The green whole-hand verdict (simulated)</h3>
				<p><span class="sw" style="background:#1c9b4f"></span><b>Whole-hand (simulated)</b> &mdash; when the
					board carries sampled data, a green band on top gives the <b>honest</b> answer: it deals out the
					unknown opponents' cards <b>many times</b> (each split weighted by how likely it is) and plays
					each whole deal out double-dummy, so it already includes entries, the tempo race, and
					cross-suit tricks (squeezes, ruffs) that the per-suit table below cannot see. Pick the
					<b>contract strain</b> with the &spades;&hearts;&diams;&clubs;NT buttons and the <b>level</b>
					with the trick slider; the band shows the make-% for that contract, with a &plusmn; margin and
					the number of deals (a simulation, so it is an estimate, not exact). The <b>reconciliation</b>
					strip &mdash; <i>ceiling &middot; blind &middot; simulated</i> &mdash; lines the three answers up:
					the gap from the per-suit ceiling down to the simulated number <b>is</b> the entry/tempo/no-squeeze
					tax. Trust the green number for "will it make?"; the suit rows tell you "how to play".</p>
				<p>The simulated make-% is still a <b>ceiling</b>: every deal is played double-dummy, so declarer
					never misguesses. When the two known hands contain a genuine <b>two-way guess</b> (a trapped
					honour finessable either way, which a blind declarer must guess), the band adds an
					<b>achievable</b> figure &mdash; the make-% docked for guessing that honour wrong half the time.
					The drop from make-% to achievable is the <b>guess tax</b> (shown as e.g. <i>Q&spades; guess,
					&minus;35</i>). It appears only on the exact contract it was estimated for, and only with no
					opening lead picked.</p>
				<p>The <b>lead</b> menu conditions that make-% on the <b>opening lead</b> (a defender holding that
					exact card) &mdash; the honest "if the king is onside" swing. It re-averages only the sampled
					deals consistent with the lead, so the deal count (and the &plusmn;) shrinks; the note reads
					<i>given E led K&spades;</i>.</p>
				<h3>The "tricks &ge;" slider</h3>
				<p>Sets the target: the chance of taking <b>at least</b> that many tricks. E.g. set it to 9 for a
					game in no-trumps, 10 for a major-suit game. The headline shows the ceiling (DD) and realistic
					(SD) chance of reaching it.</p>
				<h3>N/S vs E/W</h3>
				<p>The two buttons at the top switch <i>whose</i> tricks are analysed &mdash; your side or the
					opponents'.</p>
				<h3>IMPs vs Matchpoints</h3>
				<p><b>IMPs</b> (teams): just <i>make the contract</i>. The recommended play is the safest one for
					the target you set &mdash; overtricks barely matter, going down is costly.</p>
				<p><b>MPs</b> (pairs / matchpoints): you're ranked against everyone else in your contract, so
					<i>overtricks win boards</i>. The tool then chases the extra trick while it stays better than
					even &mdash; it aims for the <b>highest</b> number of tricks whose chance is still at least
					50%. The headline shows that "aim" (and still tells you the plain make chance).</p>
				<h3>Winner count &amp; combinations</h3>
				<p>Under the board number the panel shows your <b>sure tricks</b> &mdash; the tricks guaranteed on
					<i>every</i> layout &mdash; and, when that falls short of the slider target, how many more you must
					<b>develop</b>. A <span style="color:#b8860b">ⓘ</span> beside a suit's <b>line</b> means the
					suit is a named <b>combination</b> (a two-way finesse, 8-ever/9-never, &hellip;); hover the line
					word to read it. With sampled data the green band also flags the <b>worst opening lead</b> &mdash;
					the defender card that beats the contract most often.</p>
				<h3>Known opponent length (vacant space)</h3>
				<p>The default figures assume you know <i>nothing</i> about the opponents' shape: every split of their
					cards is weighted only by how likely it is a priori. Often you know more &mdash; a defender
					pre-empted, showed out, or the bidding marked length. The <b>&ge;</b> button (top right, on boards
					that support it) opens a little grid: two rows, one per defender, four suit columns. Type the
					<b>fewest</b> cards a defender is known to hold in a suit &mdash; e.g. a <b>3&hearts;</b> opener sitting
					East is "E &hearts; &ge; 6".</p>
				<p>The whole table then re-weights for it. Knowing one defender is <i>long</i> in a suit makes them
					<i>short</i> elsewhere, so a missing queen is more likely with their partner &mdash; and the tool
					shifts every suit's odds and <b>re-picks the blind line</b> to match (a finesse can reverse
					direction, or give way to cashing out). It reasons from a <i>minimum</i>, not an exact count, so
					"&ge; 5" correctly covers 5, 6, 7&hellip;. Enter both defenders' minimums in a suit and it couples
					them. If your numbers can't both be true (they need more cards than the suit holds) the panel says so
					and keeps the plain table. <b>clear</b>, or blanking the cells, returns to the a-priori figures;
					moving to another board clears them automatically (they belong to one holding).</p>
				<h3>Panel layout</h3>
				<p>Two buttons at the top right resize the panel when it crowds the cards on a small screen:
					<b>&#8596;</b> <i>narrow</i> caps the width so it stops covering the hands (the trick table then
					scrolls sideways inside), and <b>&#9662;/&#9656;</b> <i>collapse</i> folds the per-suit rows away,
					leaving just the totals. Both are remembered between boards.</p>
				<h3>The small print</h3>
				<p>It treats the four suits independently and assumes you can always get to the right hand ("free
					entries"). Real play has entry problems and suits interact, so treat the numbers as a
					<b>guide</b>, not a guarantee.</p>
				<p><b>Why the total can read too high &mdash; tempo.</b> The tool lets you take all your tricks
					<i>without ever handing the lead to the opponents</i>. Real bridge isn't like that: to set up
					extra tricks you often have to <b>lose the lead first</b> (a finesse that fails, conceding an
					early round). The moment the defenders are in, they <b>cash their own winners</b> &mdash; and
					they may beat you before you ever run yours. Adding up each suit's best case ignores this race
					for the lead, so a hand that looks like "13 tricks" can really be fewer once the opponents grab
					the lead and cash an ace (or a whole suit) of their own. The suit-by-suit figure is an
					<i>upper bound</i>; it does not know who gets in first.</p>
				<p>The double-dummy <b>par</b> caption on the board is the honest whole-deal number &mdash; it
					plays all four suits together <i>with</i> entries and tempo, so when combo and par disagree,
					trust par. Combo's job is the per-suit "what's available", not the race.</p>
		  </div>
	 </div>
	 <div class="cca-tip" id="nc-cca-tip" hidden></div>
	 <script>
	 (function () {
		  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
		  // This page also runs OUTSIDE a browser: the bidding-system desktop workbench loads it into a
		  // Sciter <frame> (the `@media sciter` block in the stylesheet is the layout half of that). Sciter
		  // runs QuickJS with a W3C-shaped DOM, and almost everything below is shared unchanged —
		  // `getBoundingClientRect`, `offsetWidth`, `classList.toggle(name, on)`, `closest`, `querySelectorAll`
		  // (a real Array), `tagName`, `element.click()`, delegated `mouseover`/`mouseout`/`keydown`, and
		  // DOM0 `onclick`/`oninput` handlers are all present and were measured working there.
		  //
		  // Three differences, each wrapped here rather than branched on at the call sites:
		  //
		  //   * `element.hidden` is not implemented as a PROPERTY. Assigning it is silent and inert: the
		  //     element stays visible, because it is the `[hidden]` ATTRIBUTE that the engine styles. Going
		  //     through the attribute is correct in a browser too, so there is no host test here.
		  //   * `window.innerWidth` / `innerHeight` do not exist. `document.documentElement.clientWidth` is
		  //     the viewport in both (in a browser it excludes the scrollbar, which is what this code wants
		  //     anyway — it is clamping a tooltip inside the visible area).
		  //   * `localStorage` does not exist (Sciter's store is the `@storage` MODULE, which a classic
		  //     script cannot import). The two view preferences below are read and written inside
		  //     try/catch, so on the desktop they simply do not persist between runs. Nothing to shim.
		  function setHidden(el, on) {
				if (!el) return;
				if (on) el.setAttribute('hidden', ''); else el.removeAttribute('hidden');
		  }
		  function isHidden(el) { return !el || el.hasAttribute('hidden'); }
		  // Which engine this document is running in. `Window.this` is Sciter's own global and a browser has
		  // no such thing (`Window` there is the interface object, whose `.this` is undefined), so this is a
		  // one-expression test with nothing to keep in step. Used for the two places where the desktop needs
		  // a DIFFERENT ELEMENT or a DIFFERENT NUMBER, not merely different CSS — everything expressible in
		  // CSS lives in the `@media sciter` block instead.
		  var DESKTOP = !!(typeof Window !== 'undefined' && Window.this);
		  function viewW() { return document.documentElement.clientWidth; }
		  function viewH() { return document.documentElement.clientHeight; }
		  // Which event a control raises for "the user changed me" is the one place the two engines were
		  // not measurably identical (a slider drag cannot be synthesised in a test), so the handlers that
		  // matter are registered for both names. Every one of them re-renders from the control's CURRENT
		  // value, so a browser firing `input` and then `change` runs the same work twice and lands in the
		  // same place.
		  function onEdit(el, fn) {
				if (!el) return;
				el.addEventListener('input', fn);
				el.addEventListener('change', fn);
		  }
		  // MOVING THE CAROUSEL. The viewport is a scroll container and a step is one `scrollTo` — the
		  // ANIMATION IS DECLARED IN CSS on both engines (`scroll-behavior: smooth` in a browser,
		  // `overflow-x: ... scroll-manner(animation: true)` in the `@media sciter` block), so nothing here
		  // drives a tween frame by frame. `behavior: 'instant'` is the documented override for a move that
		  // must not animate.
		  //
		  // This replaced a hand-written `morphContent` tween on the track's `transform`, and the reason is
		  // worth keeping: a transform is PAINT-TIME and unreadable. No geometry API reports it, so the
		  // position had to be re-derived from the inline style, the compensation for parking's layout change
		  // compounded into it, a superseded tween could win the last write, and nothing clamped the result —
		  // between them those produced boards flush left, boards half a screen off, boards off the end
		  // entirely, and a viewport showing the gap between two boards while the engine caught up. A scroll
		  // offset is a number the engine clamps and hands back, which is why this version can be checked
		  // arithmetically instead of by sampling pixels.
		  //
		  // Measured on 6.0.4.9 (docs: `DOM/Element/README.md` for `scrollTo`, `css/properties.md` +
		  // `samples.css/scrollbars-n-scrolling/` for `scroll-manner`): `scrollLeft` and
		  // `scrollTo({left, behavior: 'instant'})` take effect immediately and read back exactly;
		  // `scrollTo({left})` with no behavior is animated by the CSS; an in-flight animated scroll RE-TARGETS
		  // when a new one is issued (which is what a burst of clicks needs, and what the old tween could not
		  // do without falling behind); and parking a board shifts `offsetLeft` while `scrollLeft` stays, both
		  // of them readable, so the compensation is arithmetic rather than guesswork.
		  var viewport = document.querySelector('.viewport');
		  var wantedScroll = 0;

		  // Side padding, in pixels, so the FIRST and LAST boards can reach the middle of the viewport: a scroll
		  // stops at 0 and at the end, so without it they sit against the edges (measured 430px off centre).
		  // Exactly `(viewport - board) / 2` and no more — half a viewport (the usual `padding: 0 50%` trick)
		  // adds a whole viewport of scroll range that contains nothing, which is visible and wrong: with a bar
		  // on screen it can be dragged into that emptiness, and the range reads as if the boards were only the
		  // first half of the strip. Not in the stylesheet at all, because a PERCENTAGE padding on a
		  // `max-content` box is circular and the desktop engine drops it (measured: board 1 stayed 430px off).
		  function padTrack() {
				if (viewport.clientWidth <= 0) { return; }   // see refit(): nothing to divide yet
				var w = 0;
				for (var j = 0; j < slides.length; j++) { w = Math.max(w, slides[j].offsetWidth); }
				var pad = Math.max(0, Math.round((viewport.clientWidth - w) / 2));
				track.style.setProperty('padding-left', pad + 'px');
				track.style.setProperty('padding-right', pad + 'px');
		  }

		  // Pad, then put the current board back on the middle. Deferred as well as immediate, because the
		  // script does NOT always run against a laid-out document: hosted in the workbench's Sciter <frame>
		  // the frame had no size yet when this ran, so `clientWidth` was 0, the padding came out 0 and the
		  // ONE board of an analysed deal sat flush against the left edge for the life of the window — a
		  // resize is what fixed it, and a desktop window nobody resizes never got one. A board is centred by
		  // the padding alone when there is only one of it (there is nothing to scroll), which is why this is
		  // not merely cosmetic. Cheap either way: two extra measurements on a laid-out page, and the second
		  // one is what a late layout needs.
		  function refit() {
				padTrack();
				var s = slides[idx];
				if (s) scrollToPx(centreScrollFor(s), true);
		  }

		  // How far the viewport would have to scroll to put an element's middle on its own middle.
		  //
		  // Measured with RECT CENTRES, and both halves of that matter. `offsetLeft` cannot be used because the
		  // two engines disagree about it: in the desktop engine it is relative to the SCROLLED viewport (board
		  // 1 read 179 with `scrollLeft` at 461, and boards scrolled off the left read NEGATIVE), while a
		  // browser gives the unscrolled offset parent — the same expression then centres in one engine and
		  // misses by a scroll offset in the other, which is exactly what it did. A
		  // `getBoundingClientRect` is viewport-relative in both. And the CENTRE rather than the left edge,
		  // because the board's zoom is a `scale()` about its middle: a browser's rect includes that transform,
		  // so an edge moves while the tween runs and the middle does not.
		  function centreScrollFor(el) {
				if (!el) return viewport.scrollLeft;
				var r = el.getBoundingClientRect(), v = viewport.getBoundingClientRect();
				var want = viewport.scrollLeft + (r.left + r.right) / 2 - (v.left + v.right) / 2;
				var most = Math.max(0, viewport.scrollWidth - viewport.clientWidth);
				return Math.max(0, Math.min(most, Math.round(want)));
		  }

		  // An element's middle, relative to the viewport's middle: the one measurement parking has to preserve.
		  function offCentre(el) {
				var r = el.getBoundingClientRect(), v = viewport.getBoundingClientRect();
				return (r.left + r.right) / 2 - (v.left + v.right) / 2;
		  }

		  function scrollToPx(px, immediately) {
				// This is OUR move: remember where it is going, and drop any standing claim that the user is
				// scrolling. Without the second half, a click on the page (which sets that claim) left it set for
				// good, and then the settle handler below would read our own animated scroll as a user scroll —
				// firing 140ms in, while the board is still travelling, finding the board we came FROM as the
				// nearest, and putting it back. The arrow key then does nothing at all, which is what it did.
				userScrolled = false;
				wantedScroll = px;
				if (px === viewport.scrollLeft) return;
				if (immediately) {
					 // `scrollLeft =` is the one both engines apply synchronously; the option form is the
					 // documented way to say "not animated" where a stylesheet says otherwise.
					 viewport.scrollLeft = px;
					 if (viewport.scrollLeft !== px && viewport.scrollTo) viewport.scrollTo({ left: px, behavior: 'instant' });
					 return;
				}
				if (viewport.scrollTo) { viewport.scrollTo({ left: px }); return; }
				viewport.scrollLeft = px;
		  }

		  // Only a few boards stay IN THE LAYOUT. A 48-deal page is 48 boards of ~60 elements each, and every
		  // one of them is re-laid-out on every window resize step whether or not it can be seen: measured in
		  // the desktop host at 48 boards, 124ms PER RESIZE STEP, which is a window edge nobody can drag.
		  // `display: none` on the boards outside a small window round the active one takes that to 6ms, and it
		  // is work a browser was doing for nothing too. `visibility: hidden` does NOT help (measured: 119ms) —
		  // the boards still take part in layout, which is the whole cost.
		  //
		  // The window keeps the neighbour either side laid out, so a step never waits for layout.
		  // Measured in the window: 3 laid-out boards feel exactly like 5, and a narrow window feels like a wide
		  // one. So this is a LAYOUT budget (the 48-board resize cost it was introduced for), not a smoothness
		  // one — per-frame cost in this engine does not scale with what is on screen.
		  var LAYOUT_WINDOW = 2;
		  function parkFarSlides(i) {
				for (var j = 0; j < slides.length; j++) {
					 var wanted = Math.abs(j - i) <= LAYOUT_WINDOW;
					 var parked = slides[j].style.getPropertyValue('display') === 'none';
					 if (wanted === parked) slides[j].style.setProperty('display', wanted ? '' : 'none');
				}
		  }

		  var track = document.getElementById('nc-track');
		  // Each accepted deal wrote a .compass and (with --dd) one or more following caption siblings
		  // (a .par par-summary, a .combo trick table, ...). Group each compass with every sibling up to
		  // the next board into one .slide, so the carousel moves them together.
